---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, FEEDBACK_EMAIL, SOURCE_URL } from "../lib/site";

// Checked against the code on 2026-11-21: apps/macos (Sync/, SyncCrypto/, LangdockClient.swift,
// WritingReview.swift, write-info.py, Feedback.swift) and server/ (PROTOCOL.md, migrations, wrangler.jsonc, src/feedback).
// "AI tools you connect" checked on 2026-11-22 against server/src/mcp and docs/mcp.md.
// Update this page whenever sync, sign-in or Ask Matilde changes what leaves the Mac.
const updated = "22 November 2026";

const canSee = [
  "Your email address, so you can sign in and get sign-in codes.",
  "That you have an account, and when you created it.",
  "Your devices: what kind they are (for example macOS), when they signed in, and the day each one was last used. Their names are encrypted.",
  "For each synced item: a random ID, what kind of item it is (a note, a folder, a comment), how big it is, when it changed, which of your devices changed it, and whether it was deleted.",
  "If you connect an AI tool: its name, what you allowed it to do, when the connection ends, and when and how it was used (the action, never what it read or wrote).",
];

const cantSee = [
  "Your titles, your writing and your goals.",
  "Your folder names, images, stash and comments.",
  "Your conversations with Matilde.",
  "Your Matilde key, or the key that unlocks your notes.",
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Matilde"
  description="What stays on your Mac, what sync can and can't see, and what happens when you ask Matilde."
  image="/og-image-privacy.png"
  imageAlt="How privacy works: your notes are encrypted on your devices, so no one else can read them."
  markdown="/privacy.md"
>
  <main class="prose-quiet mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-14 sm:pt-20">
    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 font-sans text-[14px] text-muted hover:text-ink">
      <img src="/icon.png" alt="" width="28" height="28" class="size-7" />
      Matilde
    </a>

    <h1 class="mt-10 text-[40px] font-medium leading-tight tracking-[-0.015em]">How privacy works</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[19px] text-ink/85">
      Matilde is a small side project. This page says plainly what happens to your writing. Matilde is open
      source, so you can <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={SOURCE_URL}>check the code</a> yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">On your Mac</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Your writing is ordinary Markdown files in a folder you choose on your Mac. Goals, comments, draft history and
      your conversations with Matilde are kept in a small database on your Mac too. Nothing leaves your Mac unless
      you turn on sync, use Ask Matilde or send feedback.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      To look for updates, Matilde downloads a small file through matilde.page from GitHub, where the app is
      published. That request doesn't include your writing, and Matilde doesn't send a profile of your Mac with it.
      When you visit this site or download Matilde, or Matilde checks for updates, only an anonymous count is kept,
      with no personal data. For an update check, the count records only the Matilde and macOS version.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Sync</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Sync is optional, off until you turn it on, and still in beta. You sign in with your email address and a
      six-digit code. There's no password.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Before anything leaves your Mac, it's encrypted with a key that only your devices have (end-to-end encryption,
      AES-256-GCM). The key is kept in your Mac's Keychain. The server stores your encrypted notes and copies of the
      key that are locked with your Matilde key, which the server never gets. No one else can read your writing, not
      even someone who broke into the server. The one exception is an AI tool you choose to connect, described{" "}
      <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="#ai-tools">below</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-muted">What the server can see</h3>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-[16px]">
          {canSee.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-sans text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-muted">What it can't see</h3>
        <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-[16px]">
          {cantSee.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[16px] text-ink/85">
      The server doesn't store your IP address. To stop people trying lots of sign-in codes, a scrambled form of part of
      it is counted for a short while. The server's logs record which kind of request came in and whether it worked, not
      what was in it. Cloudflare, which runs the server, handles connection details such as IP addresses to deliver
      requests.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Where it's stored</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      The sync server runs on Cloudflare. Your account and your encrypted notes are stored in Cloudflare's EU
      locations. On the way there, requests travel through Cloudflare's network over an encrypted connection, so they
      may pass through a data centre near you. Sign-in emails are sent by Cloudflare too.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Your Matilde key</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      When you turn on sync, Matilde shows you your Matilde key once: 12 words. Write them down or keep them in your
      password manager.
      You'll need it to set up a new Mac, or if your Mac loses its copy of the key.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      This is the trade-off for privacy: if you lose all your devices and your Matilde key, no one can unlock your
      synced notes. The files on your Mac are unaffected, because they're never locked.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Deleting your account</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Deleting your sync account removes your synced notes, devices, email address and keys from the server right
      away. Database backups are kept for up to 30 days, so your email address and encrypted notes can stay in them
      until they expire. They never contain readable writing. You can't delete your account from
      inside the app yet. Until you can, write to <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
      to have it deleted.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Ask Matilde</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Ask Matilde is optional and off until you add your own Langdock API key and choose a model in Settings. Langdock
      is the AI service that gives Matilde its answers. Your key stays in your Mac's Keychain.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      When Matilde reads your page, Matilde sends it to Langdock: the title, the goal and the whole text of the draft
      you're on, with images and file locations on your Mac left out. When you talk about a passage, the conversation
      goes with it. Other drafts and your stash aren't sent.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      That happens when you ask, and on its own after you've changed the draft and paused for about thirty seconds.
      You can pause that from the Ask Matilde menu. Just opening a draft never sends anything.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      This text is not covered by sync's encryption: Langdock and the AI model you picked have to read it to
      answer. Langdock uses its EU region unless you change it, and passes the text to the model's provider. Their own
      privacy terms apply to it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ai-tools" class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">AI tools you connect</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      You can let an AI tool you use, such as Langdock, Claude, ChatGPT or Cursor, read, search and add to your synced notes.
      This is off until you connect a tool yourself, and nothing changes if you never do.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      You connect a tool on a Matilde page in your browser. You choose what it may do (read, comment, create notes,
      and, only if you tick it, edit them) and for how long: 1, 7, 30 or 90 days. You type your Matilde key on that
      page, and it stays in your browser.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Here is the trade-off. The tool talks to the Matilde server, not to your Mac, so while a tool is connected the
      Matilde server can decrypt your notes to answer it. It does that only for the tool's requests, in memory, and
      keeps no readable copy. The limits on what the tool may do are rules the server follows: while a connection is
      open, the server could decrypt all of your synced notes.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Whatever the tool reads goes to the company behind it (for example Langdock, Anthropic, OpenAI or Cursor), and their own
      privacy terms apply to it. Comments a tool leaves are marked with its name in Matilde.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Your connected tools are listed in Matilde's Settings, under Sync, on your Mac and iPhone. Disconnecting one stops
      it at once and deletes the copy of your key the server held for it. It doesn't undo what the tool has already
      read. The server keeps a short record of each connection's activity: when a tool was used and which action it
      took, never what it read or wrote.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">Feedback</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      Send Feedback (in the Help menu and in Settings) sends only what you type in it: your message, what kind of
      feedback it is and, if you add one, an email address for a reply. The app version and the kind of device are
      included too: the version and build of Matilde, the system version (for example macOS 15.2) and the device model
      (for example Mac14,2), never its name. Your notes and your account email are never included.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      It goes through the sync server to Linear, the tool used to track Matilde's bugs and ideas, where it becomes
      an issue. If Linear can't be reached, the server keeps it until it can and then deletes its copy.
      You can also write to <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={`mailto:${FEEDBACK_EMAIL}`}>{FEEDBACK_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-[24px] font-semibold">This website</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[17.5px]">
      No cookies, no analytics and no tracking. The fonts are served from this site. It's hosted on Cloudflare, which
      handles the requests.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-12 font-sans text-[13px] text-muted">Last updated {updated}.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
